  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "So this idea comes from what I saw with Half-Life: Alyx. The game had settings for spectators (desktop capture) to enable different levels of motion smoothing to make the viewing experience easier to watch, eliminating the small movements and jitters of wearing a headset normally. Smoothing those movements out makes it easier for the viewer to watch the first person perspective of the user. \nWe have this already with the camera system we have in game sure, but having this as an option for the desktop capture directly outside of the camera could be useful for content creators of any kind. Being able to have smooth video directly from the desktop capture and not a camera makes it easier to remain focused on the game and not having to worry about where the camera is located, who's in the shot, is it clipping into somebody, the list can go on. It would also let creators make trailers for worlds or other kinds of content in the first person and not worry about a camera to get the same effect. Something to think about!",
